::: {.doc-subject}

Place the nodes in layers by breadth-first distance from a start node.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L5495-L5595){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.layout_bfs(
    source,
    align="vertical",
    scale=1.0,
    center=(0.0, 0.0),
    attr_name=None,
    return_dict=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![BFS layout](../assets/graphs/layout-bfs.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="On the left, an eight-node graph scattered at random, node 1 outlined as the start. On the right, layout_bfs(source=1) puts node 1 in the leftmost column and every other node in a column by its distance from node 1, so the graph reads as layers of a breadth-first search." width="100%"}

The start node forms the first layer, its neighbors the second, their unvisited
neighbors the third, and so on; the layers are then drawn as in
`layout_multipartite()`, side by side (or one above another with
`align="horizontal"`), centered and scaled so the largest coordinate is `scale=`.
Within a layer, nodes keep the order the search reached them in, visiting neighbors
in ID order. Directed graphs are searched along edge directions. This matches
NetworkX's `bfs_layout()`.

Breadth-first layers make trees and shallow hierarchies readable and show how far
each node is from a chosen root, such as a source in a flow network or a seed
account in a social graph. Every node must be reachable from `source=`.

Positions are stored as `[x, y]` lists in the `attr_name=` node attribute; see
`layout_circular()`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Node ID or label to start from.

<code>[align]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;vertical&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   `"vertical"` (default) for vertical layers side by side, or `"horizontal"` for
    horizontal layers one above another.

<code>[scale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1.0]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Largest coordinate after rescaling. Default is `1.0`.

<code>[center]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [tuple[float, float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [(0.0, 0.0)]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Center of the layout, as `(x, y)`. Default is the origin.

<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Node attribute to store the positions in. If `None` (default), uses `"pos"`.

<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, returns a dictionary instead of self. If `False` (default), stores the
    positions on the nodes and returns self for chaining.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Union[dict[int, tuple[float, float]], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: dictionary mapping node IDs to `(x, y)` tuples. If
    `return_dict=` is `False` (default): returns self for method chaining.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If some node can't be reached from `source=`, `align=` is unknown, or `scale=` or
    `center=` is invalid.

<code> [NodeNotFoundError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `source=` doesn't exist.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A binary tree laid out from its root, one level per row:

::: {#51ffdb9e .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

ec.Graph().add_balanced_tree(2, 3).layout_bfs(source=1, align="horizontal").plot(
    layout="pos"
)
```

